  "description": {
    "ja": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 日本語版「トウゴマ」",
      "source_url": "https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E3%83%88%E3%82%A6%E3%82%B4%E3%83%9E",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 108018350,
      "text": "トウゴマ は、トウダイグサ科トウゴマ属の多年草。別名、ヒマ（蓖麻）。"
    },
    "en": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Ricinus」",
      "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ricinus",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 1378354187,
      "text": "Ricinus communis, the castor bean or castor oil plant, is a species of perennial flowering plant in the spurge family, Euphorbiaceae. It is the sole species in the monotypic genus, Ricinus, and subtribe, Ricininae."
    }
  },
  "sections": {
    "cultivation": {
      "en": {
        "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Ricinus」の節「Cultivation」",
        "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ricinus#Cultivation",
        "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
        "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
        "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
        "revision": 1378354187,
        "heading": "Cultivation",
        "text": "It is also used extensively as a decorative plant in parks and other public areas, particularly as a \"dot plant\" in traditional bedding schemes. If sown early, under glass, and kept at a temperature of around 20 °C (68 °F) until planted out, the castor oil plant can reach a height of 2–3 metres (6.6–9.8 ft) in a year. In areas prone to frost it is usually shorter, and grown as if it were an annual. However, it can grow well outdoors in cooler climates, at least in southern England, and the leaves do not appear to suffer frost damage in sheltered spots, where it remains evergreen. It was used in Edwardian times in the parks of Toronto, Canada. Although not cultivated there, the plant grows wild in the US, notably Griffith Park in Los Angeles. \n\n■ Cultivars\nCultivars have been developed  by breeders for use as ornamental plants (heights refer to plants grown as annuals) and for commercial production of castor oil.",
        "truncated": true
      }
    }
  },
